        heading: "Practical Tips for Philadelphia Hotel Searches",
        body: "Philadelphia is one of the most affordable major cities on the East Coast for hotels. Rooms that would cost three hundred dollars a night in Manhattan often run one-fifty to two hundred here for comparable quality. The sweet spot for most visitors is the area between **City Hall** and Rittenhouse Square — central, walkable, and well-connected by both subway lines.\n\nSEPTA accepts contactless payment on subways and buses, but Regional Rail still uses a separate ticketing system. Buy Regional Rail tickets in advance at the station or through the SEPTA app to avoid a surcharge for onboard purchases. A SEPTA Key card works across all local transit modes and offers discounted fares. Philadelphia is also exceptionally walkable — the distance from City Hall to Independence Hall is about a mile along Market Street, flat the entire way. Many visitors find they use transit less than expected, particularly if their hotel is in Center City or Old City.\n\nPhiladelphia's restaurant scene has exploded in the past decade — Reading Terminal Market (adjacent to **Jefferson Station**) is the best single food destination in the city, with over eighty vendors under one roof. DiNic's roast pork sandwich and the Amish vendors' baked goods are perennial favourites. The Italian Market on 9th Street, south of Center City, is another essential food stop — it is the oldest continuously operating open-air market in the United States.\n\nHotel prices peak during major conventions at the Pennsylvania Convention Center and during university graduation weekends in May. The Army-Navy football game (typically held at Lincoln Financial Field in December) also causes a spike. The quietest and cheapest months are January through March, when the city is cold but the museums, restaurants, and transit system operate at full capacity with minimal crowds. The Philadelphia Museum of Art and the Barnes Foundation are both world-class and far less crowded than their New York equivalents."
